providing the druids with a means of guarding those nearby lands from
corruption. Its builders soon grew wary of this existance and wished
to travel on to the next. In haste, they bargained with a clan of dryads.
They were given the stones, in return for their endless vigil over them.$d$, $d$This ancient stone monolith appears to have stood here for centuries,
withstanding the years of weathering and other adverse conditions. A
blanket of bright green vines wind their way up its length, prospering
from the nearby druidic magic. Several passages are visible on the
monolith:


