  (123, 49, $d$This altar is formed from a single 10-foot slab of chiseled stone resting upon
two smaller rough boulders.  The rock is scarred and stained all over, evidence
of thousands of rituals through the centuries which took place at this
megalith.  Numerous symbols have been carved into the altar, only to be either
scratched out or eroded by time, suggesting this site has passed from the
stewardship of one tradition to another over its long life.$d$, $d$This altar is formed from a single 10-foot slab of chiseled stone resting upon
two smaller rough boulders.  The rock is scarred and stained all over, evidence
of thousands of rituals through the centuries which took place at this
megalith.  Numerous symbols have been carved into the altar, only to be either
scratched out or eroded by time, suggesting this site has passed from the
stewardship of one tradition to another over its long life.$d$),
  (123, 50, $d$The towering stone, also called a menhir, stands some 30 feet tall.  Stones
such as these were often erected by religious traditions dedicated to the Old
Gods hundreds, if not thousands, of years ago.  Often, they indicated places of
great power or channeled the energy of leylines, bands of natural energy that
crisscross the surface of the planet.  Like most menhir, this one bears no
markings indicating what its purpose is.  Whether it is a natural formation, a
man-made one, or somehow summoned directly from the bones of the earth itself
isn't immediately obvious either.  Regardless of how it arrived here, or for
what purpose, it seems quite at home in the forest.$d$, $d$The towering stone, also called a menhir, stands some 30 feet tall.  Stones
such as these were often erected by religious traditions dedicated to the Old
Gods hundreds, if not thousands, of years ago.  Often, they indicated places of
great power or channeled the energy of leylines, bands of natural energy that
crisscross the surface of the planet.  Like most menhir, this one bears no
markings indicating what its purpose is.  Whether it is a natural formation, a
man-made one, or somehow summoned directly from the bones of the earth itself
isn't immediately obvious either.  Regardless of how it arrived here, or for
what purpose, it seems quite at home in the forest.$d$),
