  (237, 81, $d$The handle of this stiletto is skillfully made from heavy steel and glazed
black ivory from a stag's antlers.  A razor-sharp blade folds out from the
handle, about a hand-and-a-half in length.  Although somewhat heavy, the
stiletto can be wielded deftly within a sleeve, making an effective assassin's
weapon.$d$, $d$The handle of this stiletto is skillfully made from heavy steel and glazed
black ivory from a stag's antlers.  A razor-sharp blade folds out from the
handle, about a hand-and-a-half in length.  Although somewhat heavy, the
stiletto can be wielded deftly within a sleeve, making an effective assassin's
weapon.$d$),
  (237, 83, $d$This ring is small, yet anything but simple.  At first, it seems like a simple
ring of intertwining gold and silver strands lit with an inner fire, but it
feels alive, and indeed upon closer inspection it is revealed that the golden
bands have the glitter of living flame, flowing like molten flame around the
silver bands.  They also glimmer, but with the power of coldest ice.  One would
expect these two opposing elements bound together would destroy each other, but
instead the flame burns brighter and the ice rests cooler because of the
opposition inherent in them.  The binding is tighter than ever, a joyous
inclusion of difference.$d$, $d$This ring is small, yet anything but simple.  At first, it seems like a simple
ring of intertwining gold and silver strands lit with an inner fire, but it
