  (237, 86, $d$It's a sweet-smelling garland woven to be worn on the head, made of hundreds of
tiny flowers, their blue and white blossoms winking out of the green mess of
stems.  Though it looks like it could fall apart at any moment, it stays
together.  Someone must have spent a very long time weaving it!$d$, $d$It's a sweet-smelling garland woven to be worn on the head, made of hundreds of
tiny flowers, their blue and white blossoms winking out of the green mess of
stems.  Though it looks like it could fall apart at any moment, it stays
together.  Someone must have spent a very long time weaving it!$d$),
  (237, 87, $d$Cast from the finest dwarven silver, this locket is beautifully carved and
decorated by wonderfully complex patterns and runes.  A tiny clasp on one side
locks the hinge on one side of the locket, enclosing the memories of those who
have passed on and become forgotten.  An aura of mourning and remoteness
surrounds the locket, flowing from the tiny portrait painted within-the face is
neither male nor female, neither old nor young.  Expressionless, the face
nevertheless seems to be lonely, crying for remembrance.$d$, $d$Cast from the finest dwarven silver, this locket is beautifully carved and
decorated by wonderfully complex patterns and runes.  A tiny clasp on one side
locks the hinge on one side of the locket, enclosing the memories of those who
have passed on and become forgotten.  An aura of mourning and remoteness
surrounds the locket, flowing from the tiny portrait painted within-the face is
neither male nor female, neither old nor young.  Expressionless, the face
nevertheless seems to be lonely, crying for remembrance.$d$),
  (237, 88, $d$This pile of rags could be used for clothing, maybe, if one weren't morally
opposed to wearing minimal clothing and one could not afford real clothes.
Close inspection reveals the greyish-brown rags to be made of silk; it was
likely once a very fine garment.  Perhaps it has some magical properties?
There is surely no other reason these rags haven't found their way to the
rubbish heap yet.$d$, $d$This pile of rags could be used for clothing, maybe, if one weren't morally
opposed to wearing minimal clothing and one could not afford real clothes.
Close inspection reveals the greyish-brown rags to be made of silk; it was
likely once a very fine garment.  Perhaps it has some magical properties?
There is surely no other reason these rags haven't found their way to the
rubbish heap yet.$d$),
  (237, 89, $d$These grey-brown rags may have once been proper clothing, but now the silk is
so torn and thin that they surely can't provide much protection.  Laces on one
end could be used to attach them to a shirt like a pair of sleeves, but anyone
with fashion sense would probably rather go without sleeves at all than use
these old things.$d$, $d$These grey-brown rags may have once been proper clothing, but now the silk is
so torn and thin that they surely can't provide much protection.  Laces on one
end could be used to attach them to a shirt like a pair of sleeves, but anyone
with fashion sense would probably rather go without sleeves at all than use
these old things.$d$),
  (237, 91, $d$Barely enough to keep to the standards of decency, this pile of unsightly rags
probably used to be part of a fine suit.  Now the silk is grey-brown with age,
and tattered and shredded almost beyond recognition.  Why would anyone want to
wear this?  It hardly counts as clothing, and it surely can't be very
protective.$d$, $d$Barely enough to keep to the standards of decency, this pile of unsightly rags
