  (489, 21, ARRAY['embroidery']::text[], $d$The needlepointed designs depict fantastic birds and animals amid
twining branches and leaves. It must have taken forever to do it all.
In the corner of the tabard, needlepointed words read:

                  I am my beloved's
               And my beloved is mine.$d$),
  (490, 38, ARRAY['chapter1']::text[], $d$This chapter is entitled 'The Cult of the Griffin' and it reads:

From time immemorable griffins have lived on this island, and also those who revere
them as powerful, supernatural creatures. The cult believe that Dagon is the god of
the griffins and chooses one griffin captain to lead the other griffins during
their time on the island.  The strength of the griffins is dependant on their
captain, if he dies then the power and influence of the griffins wanes until a
new captain is appointed by Dagon.  The cult also believe that if they can
summon Dagon to appear in mortal form, as the ultimate griffin captain he will
bring ultimate power to the griffins and hence their worshippers.  The cult
worship in a chapel which contains an altar that Dagon has invested with the
power to generate new griffins.  In olden times the chamber containing the
altar was linked via a large crystal to the surface where it drew power from
the sun.$d$),
  (490, 38, ARRAY['chapter2']::text[], $d$Earle

As long as there have been griffins on this island there have also been
druids, and as long as there have been druids, there has been Earle, the
eldest druid.
Earle defeated the last griffin captain who was named Arkranos and at the
same time broke the megalith which was the conduit for the power required
by the altar. He never actually destroyed the altar though, and so the
griffins continue to thrive and there is now a rumour that a new
captain has been appointed.
There is a rumour that when Earle started to fight Arkranos he realised that
he would need assistance to completely remove the threat of griffins from
the island because the holy oak there had been contaminated with the evil.
Stories tell of a weapon that was fashioned to kill Dagon and that the
unthinkable occurred when a cutting was taken from a holy oak to help restore
balance on the isle.
The items were sent but never received.$d$),
  (490, 38, ARRAY['chapter3']::text[], $d$The future

The seer has prophesied that the cult will finally discover the means to
allow Dagon to appear in mortal form, and that at this point they shall
understand his true nature and be destroyed.$d$),
  (500, 36, ARRAY['stand', 'torch', 'torch-stand']::text[], $d$This shaped piece of marble matches the statue it stands next to.  There is a
torch standing, held in a clamp, casting light upon the statue and bathing the
room in a soft glow.$d$),
  (502, 10, ARRAY['stamp', 'customs']::text[], $d$The black ink of this stamp has run a bit, and faded. In several lines, it reads:

SPECULA PORT AUTHORITY
Export Duty
PAID$d$),
  (510, 19, ARRAY['page16']::text[], $d$A passage has been circled on this page, it reads:
    ...so in order to ensure that the magic user has a copious supply of
willing subjects for his experiments, he must first ingratiate himself with
his chosen town by generous acts. An intelligent mage will try and get
himself accepted into the council, and perhaps even move for a law which can
be used to aid him. This could state that all mad people be locked up, or
all criminals be killed. We all know how easy it is to turn a mind to madness
and the mage may then take these subjects 'under his protection' and
experiment on them without worry, as the populace soon forget what has
happened to them...

There is also a note in the margin which seems to be a calculation of some
sort and a reference to page23.$d$),
  (510, 19, ARRAY['page23']::text[], $d$Ack, its a recipe for unlimited power, but it involves several unsavoury
ingredients including human organs. A passage has been circled on this page
too, and it reads:
    ...obviously in order to get this many human hearts is impossible
which is why this spell still remains theoretical, although if one were
to perfect some sort of cloning technique, coupled with a spell for creation
of a human, then it might be possible to create a human with many vestigial
organs which would presumably still be valid for the recipe...

Your skin crawls as you continue to read, anyone who really thought this
sort of stuff would work would have to be mad right?$d$),
  (510, 19, ARRAY['page37']::text[], $d$There are some simple ciphers on the page here, and some notes in the margin
which look like the would have continued onto page 38, only that page has
been torn out of the book! You read the notes anyway and puzzle over the
missing data:
    ...and as has been known through history the simplest cipher is a
substitution whereby one letter is substituted for another (for example
the next letter in the alphabet) and the message is rewritten. The decoder
knows that the letters are shifted by a certain number of positions and
can easily decode the message. This is known as a monoalphabetic cipher. A
more complex 'polyalphabetic' cipher such as the Vigenere cipher may also
be used. The illustration on the next page shows this in action, but basically
the implementation is as follows:
     1) the keyword is chosen and repeatedly written below the phrase to be encoded
     2) the combination of keyword letter and phrase letter are used to index  the Vigenere tableau
     3) the indexed letters form the encoded phrase
And the decryption works in reverse, going down the column indexed by the
keyword letter to the encoded letter, and determining which letter heads that
row. Obviously a keyword of all 'a' would not encrypt at all.
The page ends here!$d$),
  (510, 21, ARRAY['engraving', 'engravings', 'writing']::text[], $d$It seems to be some ancient spell of containment. As you examine the construction
of the spell you realise that somehow you know the way to collapse the magic
and open the case.$d$),
  (510, 21, ARRAY['spell', 'construction']::text[], $d$You see that if you can determine the name of the spell writer, you only need
to say their name backwards to collapse the spell.$d$),
  (510, 21, ARRAY['under', 'beneath']::text[], $d$Heh, you were right, the creator couldn't resist signing their masterpiece!
It says here 'Glass engraved by Rufiaans to a pattern supplied by Shema'.$d$),
  (510, 26, ARRAY['white', 'powder', 'asbestos']::text[], $d$Hmm, the white powder seems to be asbestos.  Lovely.  Course with your
lifestyle I wouldn't think that would be the cause of your demise >;-)$d$),
  (510, 70, ARRAY['engraving']::text[], $d$You look at then engraving and notice that there is a mere one word on it.
The word "TELEPORT" is engraved deeply into the side of the chest.$d$),
  (510, 72, ARRAY['square', 'shaped', 'hole']::text[], $d$This small square shaped hole, looks like it could be used to open this chest.
But a square key?  Nonsense!$d$),
  (510, 75, ARRAY['mask', 'skin', 'human']::text[], $d$How grotesque.  Some person's face - sex now indistinguishable - was stripped
to provide this awful covering.  No hairs remain on it anywhere, and it has
been tanned to prevent deterioration and to give it a supple, smooth feel.
The eyelids are missing, and small strings are attached which go around the
wearer's ears.$d$),
  (520, 36, ARRAY['stand', 'torch', 'torch-stand']::text[], $d$This shaped piece of marble matches the statue it stands next to.  There is a
torch standing, held in a clamp, casting light upon the statue and bathing the
